---
// src/pages/about.astro — what the site is, why it exists, the rules every
// skill file follows, and what is deliberately absent. JavaScript is enhancement-only (AGENTS.md rule 4).
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="What Droid Skills is, why it exists, and the rules every skill file follows."
>
  <section class="container py-10">
    <h1 class="mb-6 font-display text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-wide">About</h1>
    <div class="skill-body">
      <h2>What this is</h2>
      <p>
        Droid Skills is a skill-file server. The product is a set of SKILL.md files —
        agent-consumable skill documents in the SKILL.md convention (a name plus a "Use when…"
        trigger description), served over plain HTTP at stable URLs: <code>/SKILL.md</code> for the
        index, <code>/&lt;skill&gt;/SKILL.md</code> for each topic. Humans get readable pages at the
        same host; agents fetch the raw Markdown with the content-type set so both render
        naturally. A machine-readable catalog also lives at <code>/llms.txt</code>.
      </p>
      <h2>Why it exists</h2>
      <p>
        Training-data staleness is the dominant failure mode for agents working on Android: the
        platform ships a major release every year, and yesterday's facts become today's bugs. The
        correction mechanism is a small set of dated, verified skill files that an agent fetches
        before it acts. This site is fully independent — it does not link to, redirect to, or
        borrow the reputation of any other site.
      </p>
      <h2>The rules every skill follows</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Dated facts only.</strong> Every time-sensitive claim names its retrieval date
          and the source page it was verified against.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Unknown prints as unknown.</strong> If a fact cannot be verified, the skill says
          so instead of guessing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Read-only by default.</strong> Inspection skills never write to the device, never
          request su, never destroy data.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Public domain.</strong> CC0 1.0 — no rights reserved. Take the files and serve
          them to your own agents.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <h2>The specification</h2>
      <p>
        The served files follow the Agent Skills specification (agentskills.io/specification,
        reviewed 2026-11-11): YAML frontmatter (<code>name</code>, <code>description</code>)
        followed by a Markdown body. Verified points of compliance: the <code>name</code> of
        every skill matches the URL directory it is served from; <code>description</code> is the
        "Use when…" trigger, well under the 1024-character limit; the optional
        <code>license</code> field (<code>CC0-1.0</code>) is emitted on every file; and every
        file sits far under the spec's 500-line body guidance. Two deviations are deliberate:
        skills are served as single files — the spec's stated minimum — rather than directories
        with <code>scripts/</code>, <code>references/</code>, and <code>assets/</code>, because
        there is nothing to execute or bundle; and cross-skill references use absolute own-host
        URLs instead of relative paths, because a fetched file cannot rely on sibling files
        sitting beside it.
      </p>
      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>
        Facts are verified against primary sources only — the official Android documentation at
        developer.android.com and the AOSP source tree at android.googlesource.com — named in
        plain text with the retrieval date, never linked. A fact that cannot be verified renders
        as unknown, or is omitted with a pointer to the command that checks it live.
      </p>
      <h2>Tooling</h2>
      <p>
        Every deploy is gated on: a type-check (<code>astro check</code>), fixture tests that
        pin the exact served frontmatter shape, a pinned-dependency audit, a full static build,
        and the specification's official reference validator (<code>skills-ref</code>) run
        against the built files agents actually fetch. Any failure blocks the deploy.
      </p>
      <h2>What is not here</h2>
      <p>
        No accounts, no email addresses, no forms, no tracking, no ads (owner ruling
        2026-11-09: no email package — this satellite has no funnel at all). Sources are named in
        plain text, not linked — an agent that can read the page can find the source. The two
        JavaScript files on the site are small enhancement scripts (one-click copy; a light /
        dark / system theme selector); every page works fully with JavaScript disabled (owner
        ruling 2026-11-10 — rule 4).
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
